{{also|précaution}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|précaution}}, {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|praecautio}}, from {{m|la|praecavere}}, {{m|la|praecautum||to guard against beforehand}}; {{m|la|prae||before}} + {{m|la|cavere||be on one's guard}}. See {{m|en|pre-}}, and {{m|en|caution}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|En-us-precaution.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Previous caution or care; caution previously employed to prevent mischief or secure good; as, his life was saved by precaution.
#* John Henry Newman
#*: The ancient philosophers treasured up their supposed discoveries with miserable '''precaution'''.
# A measure taken beforehand to ward off evil or secure good or success; a precautionary act.
#: ''to take '''precautions''' against risks of accident''

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|precautionary}}

====See also====
* {{l|en|prevention}}

